A multi-module repository (two independent modules, no go.work) where the ONLY
vulnerability in each module is a Go standard-library issue (net/http),
fixable only by bumping the go directive. The root module
is example.com/foo and the child module at sub/ is example.com/bar; both touch
GO-2024-2687 / CVE-2023-45288 (HTTP/2 CONTINUATION flood), fixed in go1.21.9.
The desired remediation bumps BOTH go directives `go 1.21.0` -> `go 1.21.9`.

EXPECTED: FAIL.

GAP: the vulncheck bot script cannot remediate standard-library vulnerabilities,
and here that failure compounds across two dimensions.

First, stdlib fixes come from the Go toolchain/go directive, not `go get` of a
module version. With GOTOOLCHAIN=local and an installed go1.26 the scan is
evaluated against the running toolchain, so govulncheck never reports the
go1.21.9 fix at all; instead it reports newer stdlib CVEs whose `Fixed in:`
lines look like `crypto/tls@go1.26.5`. The awk matches those (they contain `@`)
and the script runs `go get crypto/tls@go1.26.5`, which errors ("can't request
explicit version of standard library package"), short-circuits the
`&& go mod tidy`, and leaves the tree unchanged. (Even the genuine stdlib fix
line `Fixed in: go1.21.9` would be skipped, since it has no `@`.)

Second, the script runs `go run ... govulncheck ./...` at the REPO ROOT only.
With no go.work, `./...` covers just the root module (example.com/foo); the
child module at sub/ is never scanned, so the bot could not remediate it even
if stdlib fixes were actionable.

The real script therefore produces an EMPTY diff, while the want_diff bumps both
go directives, so the case fails.
-- go.mod --
module example.com/foo

go 1.21.0
-- main.go --
package main

import "net/http"

// net/http is the vulnerable stdlib package (GO-2024-2687); the only fix is a
// go-directive bump, which the bot cannot perform.
func main() {
	http.Get("http://example.com/")
}
-- sub/go.mod --
module example.com/bar

go 1.21.0
-- sub/handler.go --
package sub

import (
	"net/http"
)

// Client fetches a URL using net/http. Go's net/http prior to go1.21.9 is
// affected by GO-2024-2687 (CVE-2023-45288): an attacker can cause unbounded
// memory growth by flooding HTTP/2 CONTINUATION frames.
func Client(url string) (*http.Response, error) {
	return http.Get(url)
}
-- want_diff.txt --
diff --git a/go.mod b/go.mod
index 9b981c3..d7e9510 100644
--- a/go.mod
+++ b/go.mod
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
 module example.com/foo
 
-go 1.21.0
+go 1.21.9
diff --git a/sub/go.mod b/sub/go.mod
index 4751ccc..fcedf44 100644
--- a/sub/go.mod
+++ b/sub/go.mod
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
 module example.com/bar
 
-go 1.21.0
+go 1.21.9
